Here's how to implement all three requirements:

**1 & 2. Guard navigation with a confirmation dialog** — override `OnNavigating` in `AppShell.xaml.cs`:

```csharp
// AppShell.xaml.cs
protected override async void OnNavigating(ShellNavigatingEventArgs args)
{
    base.OnNavigating(args);

    // Only intercept back navigation (Pop)
    if (args.Source == ShellNavigationSource.Pop
        && Shell.Current.CurrentPage is EditProductPage page
        && page.HasUnsavedChanges)
    {
        var deferral = args.GetDeferral();

        bool discard = await Shell.Current.CurrentPage
            .DisplayAlert("Unsaved Changes",
                           "Discard changes?",
                           "Discard", "Stay");
        if (!discard)
            args.Cancel();

        deferral.Complete();
    }
}
```

Key points:
- `GetDeferral()` is **required** for async checks — without it the navigation completes before `DisplayAlert` returns.
- `args.Cancel()` stops navigation if the user picks "Stay".

**3. Custom "Cancel" back button** — add `BackButtonBehavior` in `EditProductPage.xaml`:

```xml
<ContentPage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
             x:Class="MyApp.Views.EditProductPage">

    <Shell.BackButtonBehavior>
        <BackButtonBehavior TextOverride="Cancel"
                            Command="{Binding CancelCommand}" />
    </Shell.BackButtonBehavior>

    <!-- page content -->
</ContentPage>
```

```csharp
// In your ViewModel
[RelayCommand]
async Task Cancel()
{
    // This triggers a Pop, which hits the OnNavigating guard above
    await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
}
```

The `CancelCommand` navigates back via `GoToAsync("..")`, which fires `OnNavigating` with `ShellNavigationSource.Pop` — so the same confirmation dialog appears whether the user taps your "Cancel" button or swipes back.
